`for var i, x = xs` binds the index first and the element second, and this is
the fixture where the interpreter tier and a compiled tier meet on that.

Both orders were live at once: the checker typed the first variable as the
index while the interpreter bound it to the element. Each side was consistent
with itself, so nothing failed -- `testdata/test_forindex.sngl` even asserted
the interpreter's order, and passed, because interpolating two names
type-checks whichever way round they are.

The body here does not: `i` is only ever multiplied and `name` is only ever
concatenated, so a target that bound them the other way round would not
compile. Read the three heads below and the order is stated in each language's
own spelling -- Go's range, JS's entries(), Kotlin's withIndex() -- all three
of which yield the index first.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `for name, i :=` -- the index is the first name in the head, never the element
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `\(name, i\)` -- withIndex() yields (index, element), in that order
deny out/none/html/index.html `\[name, i\]` -- entries() yields [index, element], in that order

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main node {
    var names list[string] = ["a", "b"]
    var total = 0
    var joined = ""

    button(text="run", @click {
        for var i, name = names {
            total = total + i * 10
            joined = joined + name
        }
    })
    text(value=joined)
}

window {
    main
}
